.TH PKID 8 2026-11-19 "Peios pki 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
pkid \- run the Peios certificate authority, issuing X.509 certificates by policy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pkid
.br
.B pkid
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pkid
is the Peios certificate authority service.
It runs the machine's CAs: it keeps their online keys, issues and revokes certificates, and records every request and decision in its database.
.PP
peinit starts
.B pkid
as the
.B pkid
service, under its own virtual service account; you do not run it yourself.
.B pkid
refuses to start under any other identity, since its keys are readable by that account alone.
Run
.BR pki (8)
to manage it.
.PP
Before the first start, an administrator runs
.BR "pki setup" ,
which creates
.BR pkid 's
state directories and seeds its configuration.
.B pkid
has no authority to create either itself.
Started before setup,
.B pkid
runs but cannot record anything, and opens its database at the first configuration change after setup.
.PP
.B pkid
serves requests on a local socket, one request per connection.
It authorizes each by the access token of the process that connected, which the kernel captures as it connects, and never acts as its caller.
A request names a CA; what the caller can do there is decided by that CA's security descriptor, and whether a certificate is issued by the CA's templates and the name trees.
A request that policy permits is issued at once, held for an officer of the CA to approve, or refused.
.PP
A CA's key is kept in one of two places.
A key in
.BR pkid 's
own store, under
.IR /var/state/pki/keys ,
signs unattended.
A key sealed in a file under a passphrase, such as a root's on removable media, is never kept open:
each operation that signs with it takes the passphrase in the same request, and
.B pkid
forgets the key when the operation ends.
.PP
Configuration changes apply without a restart.
.B pkid
watches its Registry subtree and rereads it whenever it changes, and every 60 seconds while it cannot watch it.
It takes up a configuration only as a whole: while any part of it is invalid,
.B pkid
issues nothing, and says why in its events, until it is corrected.
.SH OPTIONS
.B pkid
takes no options when it serves.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program name and version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B pkid
reads its configuration from
.BR Machine\eSoftware\ePki :
.B Settings
for the whole machine,
.B Templates
for the kinds of certificate its CAs issue,
.B Names
for who can request certificates for which names, and
.B Authorities
for the CAs themselves.
It writes only
.BR Authorities ,
where it records the CAs created through it; the rest is an administrator's to change, with
.BR pki (8)
or directly.
Each value is documented in
.BR regman (1);
run
.BR "regman Machine\eSoftware\ePki" .
.SH SECURITY
Each CA's
.B CaSecurity
descriptor grants four rights:
.TP
.B Read (0x1)
See every request and certificate of the CA.
Without it, a caller sees only the requests it submitted and the certificates for them or for names it owns.
.TP
.B Request (0x2)
Submit certificate requests.
.TP
.B Officer (0x4)
Approve and deny held requests, and revoke certificates.
An officer can never approve a request it submitted itself.
.TP
.B Manage (0x8)
Change the CA, sign subordinate CAs with its key, and export its key.
.PP
The default
.B CaSecurity
lets Authenticated Users request, and Administrators and SYSTEM do everything.
Creating and importing CAs needs Manage (0x8) in
.BR "Settings ManageSecurity" ,
held by Administrators and SYSTEM by default.
.PP
A request is issued only if the caller can enroll in its template, by the template's
.BR EnrollSecurity ,
and can request each name it asks for, by the
.B RequestSecurity
of that name or the nearest name above it.
Approval checks the request again as its requester, as they were when they submitted it, so an officer's own rights never widen what is issued.
.PP
Everyone can connect to the socket; what each caller can do is decided only by these checks.
A client can check that the server it reached is
.B pkid
by its service SID, S\-1\-5\-80\-927106304\-3681130234\-3843647545\-210868499\-909250352.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B pkid
stopped serving normally, or printed help or its version.
.TP
.B 1
.B pkid
could not start, such as when it runs under the wrong identity or cannot create its socket, or its socket failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an argument was given.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/pkid/pki.sock
The control socket.
.TP
.I /var/state/pki/pkid.db
The CA database: every request, certificate, decision and revocation, with who made each.
It holds nothing secret.
.TP
.I /var/state/pki/keys
The keys of CAs kept in
.BR pkid 's
store, each readable by
.BR pkid 's
service account alone.
.SH EVENTS
.B pkid
emits these event types:
.PP
.nf
pkid.configuration.loaded
pkid.request.submitted
pkid.request.approved
pkid.request.denied
pkid.authority.changed
pkid.certificate.revoked
.fi
.PP
Each is documented in
.BR evman (1).
.SH NOTES
.BR pkid 's
service runs under its own virtual service account, whose token
.BR authd (8)
makes, so the service requires
.BR authd .
The account needs SeChangeNotifyPrivilege, and SeAuditPrivilege to emit its events; without SeAuditPrivilege,
.B pkid
works but each event fails with a message on standard error.
It must be able to read the attributes of every directory on the path to its database, as SQLite checks each when it opens it.
.SH READINESS
.B pkid
tells peinit it is ready once its socket is listening, even while its configuration is invalid, so that it can answer and say what is wrong.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR evman (1),
.BR regman (1),
.BR authd (8),
.BR pki (8)
.PP
https://learn.peios.org/peios/using-peios/certificate-authority/overview
